{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>When a man has a wayward and rebellious son.</b> 'Wayward' (סורר) from the language of (Hosea 4:16) ״כפרה סוררה״ — he does not accept reproof; and 'rebellious' (ומורה) — that he rebels against their words and does the opposite of his father's will." "<b>And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote</b> that two charges lie upon him: the first, that he does not honor his father and mother; and the second, that he is a glutton and a drunkard — he has transgressed the commandment of 'honor' and has transgressed what we were commanded, ״קדושים תהיו״, that we be separated from lusts. And likewise we were commanded to know HaShem, may He be exalted, in all His ways, and a glutton and a drunkard does not know the way of YHWH. And in general there is in him at present no capital sin, for he is not put to death because of the magnitude of his sin, but rather to discipline the multitudes through him; and this is the sense of ״וכל ישראל ישמעו וייראו״. And so you find in the case of the 'wayward elder' (זקן ממרא), for there is in his ruling no sin for which he would be worthy of death; rather, it is to remove contention from the Torah. And likewise the verse mentions conspiring witnesses who are put to death though they did not kill; and likewise the verse mentions the inciter (מסית), for he is put to death by his evil speech alone, even though the enticed one did not worship idols and did not heed him — rather, his death is to discipline those who remain. Until here." "<b>And they shall discipline him.</b> They flog him after they have warned him."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:00.168271288Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}